

Experiment 1

Algorithm / concept: XOR with 0 (i.e. bitwise XOR with zero does nothing)
Example: Input string “Hello world”; for each character c, compute c ^ 0. Since XOR with 0 leaves bits unchanged, the output is same as input.

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    char *s = "Hello world";
    char *p = s;
    printf("Original: %s\n", s);
    printf("After XOR with 0: ");
    while (*p) {
        char c = *p;
        char x = c ^ 0;  /* XOR with 0 */
        putchar(x);
        p++;
    }
    putchar('\n');
    return 0;
}
OUTPUT:
Original: Hello world
After XOR with 0: Hello world


Experiment 2
Algorithm / concept: Bitwise AND / XOR with 127 (0x7F)
•	c & 127 will mask off the highest bit (bit 7) — i.e. keep only lower 7 bits.
•	c ^ 127 will flip all bits in the lower 7 bits (if ASCII in 7 bits).
Example: For character 'H' (ASCII 72 = 0x48):
•	72 & 127 = 72 (0x48)
•	72 ^ 127 = 55 (0x37), which is ASCII '7'
program in C (doing both AND and XOR for each char):

#include <stdio.h>
int main() 
{
    char *s = "Hello world";
    char *p;
    printf("Original: %s\n", s);
   printf("\nAfter AND with 127:\n");
    for (p = s; *p; p++)
 {
        char c = *p;
        char y = c & 127;
        putchar(y);
    }
    putchar('\n');
    printf("\nAfter XOR with 127:\n");
    for (p = s; *p; p++)
 {
        char c = *p;
        char y = c ^ 127;
        putchar(y);
    }
    putchar('\n');

    return 0;
}
When you run it, &127 often leaves text intact (if characters use only lower 7 bits), and ^127 will produce gibberish (non-printable or odd characters) depending on ASCII.

Output:
Original: Hello world
After AND with 127:
Hello world
After XOR with 127:
7_



